Operator overloading allows us to define custom behaviors for standard C++ operators (+, -, *, etc.) when used with user-defined objects.
```cpp
class Complex { public: int real, imag;
Complex operator+(Complex obj) {
  Complex temp; temp.real = real + obj.real; temp.imag = imag + obj.imag; return temp;
}};
```
Here, the `+` operator is overloaded to perform vector-like addition of two Complex objects.

**Inheritance Type:** Hierarchical Inheritance is suitable.
**Why:** Because both `Rectangle` and `Square` are types of `Shape`, they should both inherit from a base `Shape` class to share common attributes and enforce a standard interface (like the `area()` method).
```cpp
class Shape { virtual float area() = 0; };
class Rectangle : public Shape { /* getdata/printdata */ };
class Square : public Rectangle { /* Square can also inherit from Rect as a special case */ };
```
